# Rock-Paper-Scissors as a magma -- a Web UI demo. See docs/viewing.md.
# visual: svg
# loops: 0
"WHAT THIS SHOWS -- Rock-Paper-Scissors is a MAGMA: a set with one closed binary operation, and nothing else. No associativity, no identity, no inverses."
def u:fight(x, y) { if eq(mod(x - y + 3, 3), 1) { x } else { y } }  # x beats y exactly when (x - y) mod 3 == 1
t = table(:u:fight, range(3), range(3))  # table() is the outer product: all nine matchups in one call
t  # row = first choice, column = second, cell = the winner. 0 rock, 1 paper, 2 scissors
svg(t, "heatmap")  # the SAME array, colored by cell value on the viridis ramp
"HOW TO READ THE PICTURE -- it is a 3x3 grid, one square per matchup. Rows and columns both run rock, paper, scissors in that order. A square's color is who WON that matchup: dark purple = rock (0), teal = paper (1), yellow = scissors (2)."
"WHY IT IS A MAGMA -- every square holds 0, 1 or 2. The operation never produces a fourth thing, so the set is CLOSED under it. Closure is the only law a magma requires, and this is the picture of it."
"WHAT IS MISSING -- the grid is symmetric across the diagonal, so the winner does not depend on who moved first. But no row and no column reproduces the header order rock, paper, scissors, which means no element leaves every opponent unchanged: there is no identity."
"THE POINT -- closure alone buys you a magma, and a magma is the bottom rung. Everything above it -- associativity, an identity, inverses -- is an extra law this operation does not obey."
